ve to be an important  player on the international stage and  that its dictator's policies would pose  a threat to the world order certainly  the crisis triggered by the invasion of  kuwait can be regarded not only as one  of the major international crises of the  post-war period but also as a unique  event indeed it's the first major post  called war crisis to be located outside  the dimension of east west confrontation  for the first time a member of the  united nations kuwait precisely hadn't  just been invaded but totally occupied  and annexed by another causing hundreds  of thousands of displaced people  disruption of trade of two major oil  producers and worldwide economic  uncertainty if we analyze the scale of  this war in terms of casualties we find  that it's far less than that of the  major wars after 1945 think of korea  with 4.3 million dead vietnam with over  2 million the iran-iraq war with over  600 000 or lebanon with 250 000 but from  the point of view of mobilization and  armaments used this was after korea the  largest interstate conflict since world  war ii militarily we can identify three  distinctive features of it the first is  the extreme asymmetry of casualties  facilitated by the rise of new  technologies tens of thousands of  casualties on the iraqi side a few  hundred on the coalition side the second  is the particular way the conflict ended  with a sudden call for a halt by george  bush senior at a time when he could have  annihilated saddam altogether the third  and maybe most dramatic is the  ecological disaster that came with it  following the iraqi decision to set fire  to kuwait's oil fields during the  retreat you remember the famous scene  from the movie jarhead don't you  it's raining  it's running  of course it wasn't the first time that  war and ecological destruction went  hand-in-hand but in this case the  destructive action found on military  justification and was devastating to the  healthiness of the air over a large area