s questioning the boundary the  limitation between the two countries  because the way the united kingdom had  drawn its border back in 1923 prevented  iraq from controlling a proper outlet to  the persian gulf corresponding to the  disputed bubiyan and warba islands and  the piece of desert where the rumayala  deposit is located crossing the border  between the two states and always  claimed by iraq we said in the aftermath  of the ceasefire demanded by tehran  kuwait contravening the extraction quota  agreements established at opec  organization of the petroleum exporting  countries increased crude oil extraction  from the rumyla fields this episode  known as oil theft through slant  drilling which is a particular  extraction technique using non-vertical  drills brought more than 2.4 billion  dollars into the country's treasury  corresponding to about 300 000 barrels  of oil per day an action that collapsed  baghdad's revenue which was ninety  percent dependent on crude oil by about  seven billion dollars a year while  interest on the debt also amounted to  seven billion dollars in fact none of  the gulf countries came to saddam zaid  by proposing to negotiate the less  honorable forms of debt repayment  despite the rise constant requests to  ease the pressure so the invasion of  kuwait would offer a multi-layered  solution a distraction from domestic  discontent over economic mismanagement  the possibility of acquiring kuwait  assets and investments and the seizure  of the oil fields themselves which was  equivalent to controlling about one  fifth of all world oil saddam's  hostility found confirmation on an  important occasion at the arab league  meeting held on july 16 1990 iraqi  foreign minister tarek aziz delivered to  the league's secretary-general chadley  libby a memorandum in the shape of a  declaration of war accusing kuwait of  stealing oil and the remaining gulf  countries of plotting an economic  conspiracy against iraq aimed at putting  it out of business once and for all we  can say that the iraqi dictator already  supported in the past by the west was  convinced that his move would have no  practical consequences apart from the  usual ritual protests this is confirmed  by the july 25 1990 summons of u.s  ambassador epper glaspie saddam gave her  barely an hour's notice glaspy didn't  make it in time to ask the state  department for instructions and the  dialogue between the two resolved into  comedy of misunderstandings too absorbed  in the problems of the east the diplomat  implied that the us didn't want to  intrude on inter-arab disputes the iraqi  dictator convinced that it had made  these intentions clear believed he had  obtained the tacit u.s green light to  invade kuwait an enormous mistake that  would cost iraq dearly which from the  time of the coalition ground attack  dated february 24 1991 lost control of  kuwait in just four days very  controversial where the u.s airstrikes  on the night of february 26 27 directed  against iraqi forces retreating from  kuwait to the city of basra the allies  initially bumped the head and tail of  the huge column of vehicles traveling on  the so-called highway of death so as to  block the convoy and make it a static  target for subsequent attacks some  commentators suggested that the use of  force had been disproportionate since  the iraqis had agreed to withdraw in  compliance with u.n resolution 660 and  the convoy included kuwaiti prisoners  politicians and palestinian civilian  refugees in any case saddam remained in  power cracking down hard on kurdish  uprisings in the north and shiite  uprisings in the south but had to accept  the u.n resolution 687  pledging to destroy his arsenals of  unconventional weapons pay reparation  for war damage and divide by two  security zones created by the coalition  for kurds and shiites precisely the  allied victory though described by  almost all the western press as  challenging but fair with saddam hussein  compared to adolf hitler and his  fearsome army to the wermacht was easy  and with very few casualties the whole  thing as we shall see was peppered with  the campaign of mystification in the  western press to justify the military  intervention and the spectacular life  broadcast by journalist peter arnett  from baghdad's rashid hotel describing  the bombing for hours it was the first  time it happened on live television and  many including the italian historian  antonio scurati say that this  contributed to the viewers lack of  empathy for the war who from then on  became accustomed to seeing death and  bombs on the screen not as a chilling  loss of life but as an effective  destruction against the boredom the  allied victory was easy but bush was  unable or maybe unwilling to take  advantage of the internal tensions in  iraqi society to deal a decisive blow to  saddam it was felt that attained but not  ideologically fundamentalist enemy could  still come in handy later against  possible unrest caused by peoples  bordering the iraqis apparently friendly  but actually hostile we mustn't forget  then that a large section of islamic  public opinion spoke out in favor of  saddam himself seeing the coalition's  presence as unwelcome western  interference in middle eastern affairs  and damage to the holy sites of islam  saddam after having always been utterly  secular and completely indifferent to  religion knew how to sniff the wind of  protest and began to pose as a protector  of islam attending public prayers waving  the rhetoric of faith his regime  survived but internal rivalries kurdish  independence shiite discontent were  never quelled and came back to the  surface during the second gulf war  that's another story now is the time to  talk about cnn effect but before we get  there let's see how american and it's  allies intervention in a conflict that  was taking place on the other side of  the world was justified the bush  administration tried to convince world  opinion that a real threat was imminent  and the saudis that an american presence  in the gulf was inevitable on august 15  1990 it was george bush senior himself  who advanced the first rational  explanation our jobs our way of life our  freedom and that of friendly countries  around the world would suffer if control  of the world's great oil reserves fell  into the hands of that one man saddam  hussein reasons that seemed far-fetched  there was no real threat to freedom  except as historian theodore draper put  it to that freedom to drive your car on  cheap fuel but interests in the gulf  area didn't stop at oil take for example  the massive chemical arsenal in the  hands of saddam hussein historical  reconstructions show how western aid was  instrumental in the development of the  iraqi weapons program mentioning more  than 2 000 companies involved in various  ways in the formation of the iraqi war  industry washington has long denied  possessing information regarding the  chemical arsenal in the hands of the  race but recent studies prove otherwise  on august 23 2013 foreign policy  published a survey showing top secret  documents in hand that the u.s was aware  of the existence of these weapons as  early as 1984 and was constantly  monitoring saddam hussein's plans for a  chemical attack on iran which would have  banned tehran in 1988 through the use of  sarin gas and mustard gas the cia had  information that for decades remained in  the secret archives and about which the  government was always silent even  strongly condemning the use of these  instruments of war which moreover was  prohibited by the geneva protocol of  1925 ratified by the united states in  1975. prior to that survey it was known  that the us had provided iraq with  intelligence support and that there was  only suspicion that the chemical arsenal  was being used in retaliation for war  more than suspicions awareness in fact  some declassified documents extracted  from the national archives in college  park maryland show how senor us  officials had been briefed about the  extent of the nerve gas attacks as  journalist shane harris argued this  amounted to a tacit u.s admission of  complicity in one of the most gruesome  chemical weapons attacks ever launched  in open contrast to the geneva protocol  already mentioned and what about  diplomatic relations between the united  states and iraq well they were put at  risk even before the outbreak of the war  on february 15 1990 voice of america the  official radio and television service of  the us government broadcast a program  appealing to the public for a general  mobilization against authoritarian  regimes around the world iraq was  mentioned almost constantly and saddam  was portrayed as one of the worst  expressions of tyranny an apology came  immediately from washington but the  matter was never digested by the iraqi  leader an apology that glaspy also made  to him remember the american diplomat  for how he had been portrayed in the  interview conducted by diane sawyer on  abc news claiming that that was the  typical journalistic method used to  discredit any political figure but how  far did the news media go the news  outlets succeeded in legitimizing the us  military intervention in the gulf  overnight transforming saddam hussein  from a friend of the west to the  embodiment of adolf hitler endorsing  george bush senior's line of thinking  think that the new york times in an  editorial published on august 9 1990  stated that president bush has drawn a  line in the sand committing u.s forces  to face down saddam hussein the costs  and risks are enormous but on balance he  has made the right choice in the right  way saddam had officially become a u.s  problem and the proof lies in the fact  that in the time span between august 2  and november 8 1990 the washington post  and the new york times began to cover  iraq saudi arabia and kuwait constantly  the former published a total of over  1700 articles on the subject and the  latter over 2400 with a focus on news  events obviously ignoring the real  reasons for the conflict and why u.s  iraq relations had worsened in recent  years in short an apt comparison that  resonated with the people accepted by 6  out of 10 americans as reported in a  poll conducted between august 9 and 10  1990 for the new york times for the  television industry on the other hand  the gulf war was more of a story of  american prowess especially a  celebration of technological power it's  estimated that 17 of the images shown  were shots of missiles and cutting-edge  war technology consider that on january  22 1991 cnn spoke of the patriot  missiles as if they were human actors  but it was also a glorification of the  courage determination and skill of  american soldiers if with vietnam  television had brought to light the  atrocities of war something different  was happening with the gulf a narrative  marked by the opposition between good  and evil was adopted packaged within a  real show complete with custom logo and  music to accompany the rappers a show  expertly hosted by cnn the green light  for operation desert storm came when new  york was minutes away from 7 pm on  january 16 1991 the news network's prime  time on the first night of non-stop  bombing americans were addicted to the  images of war and didn't tear themselves  away from the live broadcasts on cnn  this was because shortly after the  attack on baghdad began all the networks  lost their video link from iraq the only  broadcaster to maintain it with its  reporters was precisely cnn on the  screens were the images of three  journalists work correspondent peter  arnett reporter john holliman and tv  anchorman bernard show in fact the cnn  in september 1990 had managed to obtain  permission from iraqi officials to  install a four-wire satellite phone a  device that could withstand signal loss  on january 17 all crews present in  baghdad including cnns were asked to  leave the rashid hotel headquarters of  the international press marked as a  coalition bombing target for the  following night holy man and show  decided to leave while peter arnett  remained on site the rashida hotel was  never bombed and for the next two weeks  arnett was the only us citizen  broadcasting information and interviews  from iraq via the four wire link carried  on a truck with the large cnn initials  advisable on the canopy so that  coalition planes wouldn't mistake the  vehicle for a scooted missile shipment  so by the end of the month cnn became  the only news provider for more than 100  countries the primary source of  information from the gulf to ensure that  cnn as an all news channel could  broadcast 24 7 news two military  analysts were hired on a permanent basis  former air force major general perry  mccoy smith and military strategy expert  james blackwell it was major mccoy smith  in one of his books who explained the  formula for the american broadcaster's  success cnn had the reporters the  satellite link-ups the engineers the  producers and expert commentators in  place or on standby he had a leadership  team which understood before the war  broke out that this was going to be a  story of giant proportions the job of  the analysts was to always be at the  broadcaster's back and call always ready  to comment on the images to explain the  strategic meaning of a certain bombing  and the technical details about recon  planes and missiles dropped on a variety  of targets a rather simple example  explains the power of the media in  influencing public opinion on january 23  1991 barely a week into the war major  smith during a broadcaster recommended  reading three books to better understand  the strategies behind ongoing operations  all three sold out by the same evening  and publishers rushed with reprinting  one of the three titles boomed in demand  and forced the publishers to reprint 600  000 copies however the power of cnn  effect also affected foreign policy  decisions a case study is from february  1991 and is related to kurdish refugees  who had sowed to shelter from iraqi  forces in the mountains southeast of  turkey media coverage of the kurdish  crisis was intensive emphasizing both  the precarious conditions in which the  kurds were living and they demand that  something be done to help them  the images shocked the feelings of world  leaders british prime minister john  major decided to take the issue to the  european community and convince the  first european partners and then  president bush to create humanitarian  corridors in iraq and so began what  would become operation provide comfort  aimed at getting refugees from the gulf  war to safety barely a year after the  conflict in the middle east and moving  elsewhere the united states decided  under heavy media pressure to intervene  in response to the humanitarian  emergency resulting from the somaly  civil war but in october 1993 everything  changed images of a usa soldier killed  and dragged through the streets of  mogadishu quickly went around the world  prompting president clinton to withdraw  troops from the territory john chateau  consistent secretary of state between  1993 and 1998 reveals how the media  first brought the us into somalia and  then out of the conflict according to a  so-called push and pull effect but the  ability of television to determine what  rises to the status of an international  crisis finds expressed confirmation in a  sentence authored by bill clinton on  july 17 1995 in response to the media  call for military intervention in bosnia  we have a war by cnn our position is  unsustainable it's killing the us  position of strength in the world well  even today we have come to the end i  thank you all for your attention and see  you in the next video ciao

