 maryland show how senor us  officials had been briefed about the  extent of the nerve gas attacks as  journalist shane harris argued this  amounted to a tacit u.s admission of  complicity in one of the most gruesome  chemical weapons attacks ever launched  in open contrast to the geneva protocol  already mentioned and what about  diplomatic relations between the united  states and iraq well they were put at  risk even before the outbreak of the war  on february 15 1990 voice of america the  official radio and television service of  the us government broadcast a program  appealing to the public for a general  mobilization against authoritarian  regimes around the world iraq was  mentioned almost constantly and saddam  was portrayed as one of the worst  expressions of tyranny an apology came  immediately from washington but the  matter was never digested by the iraqi  leader an apology that glaspy also made  to him remember the american diplomat  for how he had been portrayed in the  interview conducted by diane sawyer on  abc news claiming that that was the  typical journalistic method used to  discredit any political figure but how  far did the news media go the news  outlets succeeded in legitimizing the us  military intervention in the gulf  overnight transforming saddam hussein  from a friend of the west to the  embodiment of adolf hitler endorsing  george bush senior's line of thinking  think that the new york times in an  editorial published on august 9 1990  stated that president bush has drawn a  line in the sand committing u.s forces  to face down saddam hussein the costs  and risks are enormous but on balance he  has made the right choice in the right  way saddam had officially become a u.s  problem and the proof lies in the fact  that in the time span between august 2  and november 8 1990 the washington post  and the new york times began to cover  iraq saudi arabia and kuwait constantly  the former published a total of over  1700 articles on the subject and the  latter over 2400 with a focus on news  events obviously ignoring the real  reasons for the conflict and why u.s  iraq relations had worsened in recent  years in short an apt comparison that  resonated with the people accepted by 6  out of 10 americans as reported in a  poll conducted between august 9 and 10  1990 for the new york times for the  television industry on the other hand  the gulf war was more of a story of  american prowess especially a  celebration of technological power it's  estimated that 17 of the images shown  were shots of missiles and cutting-edge  war technology consider that on january  22 1991 cnn spoke of the patriot  missiles as if they were human actors  but it was also a glorification of the  courage determination and skill of  american soldiers if with vietnam  television had brought to light the  atrocities of war something different  was happening with the gulf a narrative  marked by the opposition between good  and evil was adopted packaged within a  real show complete with custom logo and  music to accompany the rappers a show  expertly hosted by cnn the green light  for operation desert storm came when new  york was minutes away from 7 pm on  january 16 1991 the news network's prime  time on the first night of non-stop  bombing americans were addicted to the  images of war and didn't tear themselves  away from the live broadcasts on cnn  this was because shortly after the  attack on baghdad began all the networks  lost their video link from iraq the only  broadcaster to maintain it with its  reporters was precisely cnn on the  screens were the images of three  journalists work correspondent peter  arnett reporter john holliman and tv  anchorman bernard show in fact the cnn  in september 1990 had managed to obtain  permission from iraqi officials to  install a four-wire satellite phone a  device that could withstand signal loss  on january 17 all crews present in  baghdad including cnns were asked to  leave the rashid hotel headquarters of  the international press marked as a  coalition bombing target for the  following night holy man and show  decided to leave while peter arnett  remained on site the rashida hotel was  never bombed and for the next two weeks  arnett was the only us citizen  broadcasting information and interviews  from iraq via the four wire link carried  on a truck with the large cnn initials  advisable on the canopy so that  coalition planes wouldn't mistake the  vehicle for a scooted missile shipment  so by the end of the month cnn became  the only news provider for more than 100  countries the primary source of  information from the gulf to ensure that  cnn as an all news channel could  broadcast 24 7 news two military  analysts were hired on a permanent basis  former air force major general perry  mccoy smith and military strategy expert  james blackwell it was major mccoy smith  in one of his books who explained the  formula for the american broadcaster's  success cnn had the reporters the  satellite link-ups the engineers the  producers and expert commentators in  place or on standby he had a leadership  team which understood before the war  broke out that this was going to be a  story of giant proportions the job of  the analysts was to always be at the  broadcaster's back and call always ready  to comment on the images to explain the  strategic meaning of a certain bombing  and the technical details about recon  planes and missiles dropped on a variety  of targets a rather simple example  explains the power of the media in  influencing public opinion on january 23  1991 barely a week into the war major  smith during a broadcaster recommended  reading three books to better understand  the strategies behind ongoing operations  all three sold out by the same evening  and publishers rushed with reprinting  one of the three titles boomed in demand  and forced the publishers to reprint 600  000 copies however the power of cnn  effect also affected foreign policy  decisions a case study is from february  1991 and is related to kurdish refugees  who had sowed to shelter from iraqi  forces in the mountains southeast of  turkey media coverage of the kurdish  crisis was intensive emphasizing both  the precarious conditions in which the  kurds were living and they demand that  something be done to help them  the images shocked the feelings of world  leaders british prime minister john  major decided to take the issue to the  european community and convince the  first european partners and then  president bush to create humanitarian  corridors in iraq and so began what  would become operation provide comfort  aimed at getting refugees from the gulf  war to safety barely a year after the  conflict in the middle east and moving  elsewhere the united states decided  under heavy media pressure to intervene  in response to the humanitarian  emergency resulting from the somaly  civil war but in october 1993 everything  changed images of a usa soldier killed  and dragged through the streets of  mogadishu quickly went around the world  prompting president clinton to withdraw  troops from the territory john chateau  consistent secretary of state between  1993 and 1998 reveals how the media  first brought the us into somalia and  then out of the conflict according to a  so-called push and pull effect but the  ability of television to determine what  rises to the status of an international  crisis finds expressed confirmation in a  sentence authored by bill clinton on  july 17 1995 in response to the media  call for military intervention in bosnia  we have a war by cnn our position is  unsustainable it's killing the us  position of strength in the world well  even today we have come to the end i  thank you all for your attention and see  you in the next video ciao
 

